Tracing the actual code: the escrow branch is entered only when a withdraw request is pending for the currently-scoped
  account, and every mutation of that account's share count is paired, in the same call, with an equal mutation of the
  request's recorded share denominator — a withdraw request is opened with the account's full sharesHeld as
  sharesAmount; the liquidation hook calls tokenizeWithdrawRequest(liquidateAccount, liquidator, sharesToLiquidator)
  with exactly the share quantity the base vault transfers to the liquidator, and the escrowed redemption path calls
  finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest(..., sharesToBurn: sharesToRedeem) with exactly the share quantity the base vault
  burns, together with the pro-rata yieldTokensBurned = w.yieldTokenAmount * sharesToRedeem / w.sharesAmount. So the
  shares argument reaching convertToAssets — the account's remaining balance — stays equal to (or a sub-portion of) the
  request's remaining sharesAmount, and the manager's pro-rata valuation is an interpolation within the request, not an
  extrapolation beyond it. There is no code path shown in which the vault reduces an account's shares without the
  manager's denominator being reduced by the same amount, nor one in which a partially-escrowed account retains
  unrequested yield tokens outside the request while the request remains pending. The claim's premise — that shares can
  exceed w.sharesAmount, or that PT remainder is held outside the request — is asserted, not demonstrated, and the
  withdraw-request-manager implementation that would have to exhibit that divergence is not part of the presented code;
  the pro-rata arithmetic that is presented is self-consistent. Under the tie-break rule this cannot survive.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C15|A claim that a valuation function mis-scales a quantity must exhibit, from the presented code, a
  concrete path where the caller-supplied amount and the accounting denominator it is divided against are updated by
  different quantities; if every mutation shown adjusts both sides by the same amount, and the divergence would have to
  occur inside an external component whose code is not presented, the mismatch is unproven.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/staking/AbstractStakingStrategy.sol:convertToAssets:51|class=C15|Every share-reducing path pairs
  the vault's share change with an identical change to the withdraw request's sharesAmount denominator
  (tokenizeWithdrawRequest(..., sharesToLiquidator) on liquidation, finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest(..., sharesToBurn:
  sharesToRedeem) with pro-rata yieldTokenAmount on escrowed redemption), so the shares forwarded to
  getWithdrawRequestValue remains within the request's own denominator and no extrapolation or discarded-remainder
  mispricing is demonstrated.
